Steven,

Well, your problem with getting service is understandable, but, I'm sad
to say, I cannot imagine it can be comprehensible to you.  The crux of
it is that the machine you have comes from Digital Semiconductor (DS),
not from the parts of Digital that sell systems (and provide technical
support for them).  What you have is a product made from a DS reference
system, which is normally sold by DS OEMs, whose responsibility it is to
provide technical support for their customers.  Now, I don't know how
WSU came by the machine, but it's likely to be an orphan, as far as
service goes, unless you can find out who the real vendor is, or you
have WSU call up Digital and order a service contract for it.  Mind, you
still should not have gotten the runaround from Digital, in any case,
and I'm sorry that happened.

You should be able easily to use COM3, or COM4, taking IRQ 7.  If you
used IRQ 1 or 12, you'd conflict with the keyboard/mouse controller; if
you used IRQ 3, you'd conflict with COM1, and IRQ 4 conflicts with COM2.
 What you might wish to do is run "winmsd" from a console prompt, and
look at what it says about the resources that are currently in use;
choose your IRQ and I/O address based on what's not there.  If you're
still having trouble after trying that, send me e-mail.

Cheers,
James

