At 04:27 pm 03/06/96 -0500, Yang-Gul Lee wrote:
>Hi Folks
>
>Now, I am using DEC ALPHA Station 250. 
>I want to run my research code as a batjob on this machine.
>I used the command "start" to run the code as a batjob.
>What I did is:
>   > start /low /b mycode.exe
>
>When I run the code with the above NT command in the MS-DOS command window,
>the batjob was working well unless I exit the MS-DOS command window.
>Whenever I exit the window, the batjob was killed immediately.
>If I don't exit, the batjob is going to the end normally.
>
>I also tested the same command after I logged on the machine remotely.
>But the result is the same. Right after I log out, the batjob is killed.
>
>Does anyone have any idea about this and please, give me a solution.
>thanks
>
>Yang-Gul Lee

The only way that you will get a job to be independent of your logon is
if the job is started by a service.  The job could be launched by the "AT"
command which would require that the "Scheduler" service is allowed
to interact with the desktop, and you will also have to use the
"/interactive" with the AT command.

The reason why your job is ending when you exit the window is because
you are using the "/b" switch which forces the new job to not create
a separate window.

Regards
