From scoop@nkn.net Sat Mar  9 17:45:06 1996 CST
Message-ID: <v02130503ad67388540cf@[206.42.180.138]>
Date: Sat, 9 Mar 1996 17:45:46 -0500
From: Shane Cooper <scoop@nkn.net>
Subject: Re: Unix vs. NT

>>Listen up People,
>>  I know that this is an AlphaNT bunch, but why did you pick NT over
>>Unix on you alpha.  No flaming please just the facts!!!
>>
>>Thanks
>>Alan

Unix vs NT
Its a philosophy more than all technical. Unix is traditionally complicated
and requires full time paid staffer to solve only that servers problems. It
is not for the faint of heart. My support philosophy is KISS (as well as
fast). I've had a unix based server (Sparq 1000, Runing Solaris), to really
work on the system required a unix only guru. (which I'm not). I refused to
hire some one full time whose only job was look after the Unix box.

My administrative philosphy is not to create job security for myself by
implementing a complex server system so only I know how to fix, rather I
prefer to install a fast server that is farely basic in function, easy to
maintain, stable and fast.

Today, I have WinNT 3.51 running on 21164, quad issued, DEC clones
purchased from a local Dallas manufacturer, their server stations will blow
any DEC product away. For our application, fast I/O is the main criteria
for a server. NT is easy to setup, configure, monitor and trouble shoot. If
I'm called during off hours, most users are savy on MacOS and Windows based
OS's so walking them through a typical minor problem is easy. WinNT allows
us to seamlessly integrate our Macs, windows and other NT servers together
all on the same ethernet/fast ethernet network. Unix requires various
integrated tool sets and thrid party software packages to do the same
functions that are built into WinNT.

IMHO

