> 

We sell Alpha-based machines that are basically focused on the 3D 
graphics market.  Here are some actual numbers from benchmarks that 
we've compiled for a couple of years.  These numbers are the times 
taken to render 4 different 3D scenes on a variety of processors.  
The scenes are sufficiently complex that they break caches and don't 
just sit there in floating point loops.  The last column compares the 
speed of the processor to that of a Pentium/100MHz.

                     Textures   Dof   Raytrace   Zbufsort   xP5/100
i80486/66MHz           1138      5173   45958     4679 sec    0.10
Pentium/90MHz           164       544    4499      411 sec    0.91
Pentium/100MHz          145       477    4276      386 sec    1.00
MIPS R4600/133MHz       133       473    3657      302 sec    1.08
Pentium Pro/150MHz       74       248    1940      211 sec    1.97
Pentium Pro/200MHz       56       188    1497      160 sec    2.59
Raptor3 Alpha45/275MHz   57       187    1550      152 sec    2.60
Raptor3 Alpha5/300MHz    28        91     877      111 sec    4.56
Raptor3 Alpha56/366MHz   26        87     781       83 sec    5.26

As you see, the Pentium Pro 200MHz renders at almost exactly the same 
speed as our Raptor 45/275MHz machine, which is between 20% and 50% 
faster than the 4/233 that you talk about.  Our 5/300MHz is a 21164 
at 300MHz.  As you can see, it's about 76% faster than the PPro.  The 
21164A-based 56/366MHz is just over twice as fast as the PPro 200.  

If you're trying to figure out relative performance at an instant in
time, it's reasonable to say that Alpha is twice as fast as Intel at
any given time.  If you're trying to figure out time delta, then you
can say that Alpha processors are about 18 months ahead of Intel, or
that you won't see an Intel processor to match the 56/366MHz
performance until about late 1997.

