From: Kevin A. Mocklin <mocklin@frodo.eng.pko.dec.com>
Subject: Re: FW: Netscape 2 Security Problem
In-Reply-To: <31445F72@Pentagon-sbmsw.army.mil>

Rick,

Can you give us any more technical info on this?  Could this be
refering to the DNS spoofing hole that Netscape has issued a patch
for?  

thanks,
Kevin
Digital Equipment Corp.

> 
> 
> 
>  ----------
> From: Glenn Mucklow
> To: Mucklow, Rick
> Subject: Netscape 2 Security Problem
> Date: Thursday, March 07, 1996 10:38PM
> 
> 
> 
> FYI:
> 
> Someone from my former employer ("MFE") just handed me a warning about
> Netscape 2.x.
> 
> It seems that a nonauthorized user at a remote site can design a response to 
> 
> your search request that downloads your directories and then ftp the ones
> that the intruder finds interesting. This is possible due to the 
> introduction
> of JAVA in Netscrape. They can also grab your list of newsgroups and URL
> directory. Access to all mounted disks (local and remote) as well.
> 
> MFE considers this to be a serious defect and is recommending that users 
> fall
> back to v1.x. Netscrape found out about this Tuesday and also considers it 
> to
> be serious -- they've promised a fix and an upgrade.
> 
> As old uncle Ted always says "don't ever buy or use version x.0 of anything, 
> 
> even towels." Thats why we should always release csp v x.1.3 -- trap the
> unwary.
> 
> ___
>

