From: Sharma, Tushar <tsharma@dialcall.com>
Subject: RE: Unix vs. NT

Alan,
     Dennis Ritchie ( of K&R C fame) says ( not an exact quote, mind you) 
that

'We created an elegant kernel in UNIX, and it requires a programmer to 
understand the elegance".

The problem, is number of end users in this world is greater than number of 
programmers, and hence the popularity of WNT/MacOS vs. UNIX. (Even though, I 
use all three - WNT/Digital UNIX/OpenVMS).

Look at how all variants of UNIX are still struggling with a simple concept 
like a good/intuitive GUI (Motif/ Open Look/CDE etc.) as compared to Windows 
or Macintosh UIs.

On the other hand UNIX has some very good stuff with DCE which WNT (on Alpha 
at least) has.

As others point out, UNIX has it's place in the universe (the universe of 
programmers)
vs. other operating systems.

For example, to find out if a file is directory, you must run a 'file' 
command. Do you need to do this in WNT/MacOS?

.Tushar.

