There are nearly 600 subscribers to this list.  Each could contribute $15   
US, I'll buy a AlphaStation 255 and lend it to NCSA.  The rest of the   
time, I'll use it to stock and manage the archive (letting my daughter   
and son have their Macintosh back). 8^)

Seriously though, this sounds like what happened with Kermit.  It's   
simply a matter of the right people knowing that there's a need.  Someone   
inside Digital who has the authority to lend a machine simply hasn't been   
made aware of the need by NCSA.  The people inside NCSA may not be asking   
the right people.  Note the key words: "authority", "aware", and "right".   
 Digital wants to do the right thing.  If enough people want the latest   
rev of Mosaic or Netscape or Kermit, we need to let them (Digital & the   
developer) know.  Both parties need to understand the importance of the   
application to us users.  It's not fair for Digital to have to give away   
hardware to software developers, but if Digital wants Alpha to succeed,   
oftentimes that's what's required, especially during these early days of   
a new technology.  It's a delicate balancing act needed to ensure that   
all the right packages are ported, and that developers are prioritized.

How would you like to have to give away a few million dollars of Alphas?   
 Why can't NCSA buy a Polywell 275 for $3995?  Or a noname box for $1495?

Let's not jump on Digital for not seeing the need for Mosaic.  Give them   
credit for working with Netscape (even if DECdirect doesn't know about   
the package yet...  grrrrr)

The best way we can help is by voting with our dollars.  If Netscape is   
available, buy it.  If Kermit is shareware, pay the fee.  If SLnet's   
evaluation period has expired and you still need it -- don't reinstall   
it, buy it.

Oops, down boy.  Off my soap box now.

Aaron
